Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 21:45:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/1] HID: nintendo: skip baudrate setup for 8BitDo adapters

The 8BitDo USB Wireless Adapter, when switched into Switch mode, presents
itself with Nintendo's USB IDs (057e:2009) and is therefore claimed by
hid-nintendo. It does not implement the JC_USB_CMD_BAUDRATE_3M command,
and reacts to receiving it by entering an error state in which it resets
constantly: the device disconnects and re-enumerates in a tight loop,
every subsequent handshake fails with -EPROTO, and the probe eventually
gives up leaving the HID device bound to no driver at all -- not even
hid-generic, because hid_generic_match() declines while a specialised
driver still matches the ID table. The gamepad is unusable.

The adapter cannot be identified from its USB descriptors: the VID:PID,
the manufacturer and the product strings are all copied from a genuine
Switch Pro Controller, so a hid-quirks entry keyed on 057e:2009 would
break real Pro Controllers. It can, however, be identified from its
Bluetooth MAC address, which carries 8BitDo's E4:17:D8 OUI and is
returned by the JC_SUBCMD_REQ_DEV_INFO subcommand.

Move the joycon_read_info() call ahead of the USB baudrate/handshake
block so the MAC address is known before the baudrate command would be
sent, and skip that block entirely for devices whose MAC identifies them
as 8BitDo hardware.

Moving the block out of the "first handshake succeeded" arm would silently
drop an existing fallback: a USB device that does not answer the first
handshake previously fell through untouched and probed at the default baud
rate, whereas running the block unconditionally would send it a second
handshake it was never going to answer either, and that second failure is
fatal. Record the first handshake's result in usb_handshook and gate the
block on that, so the only behavioural change is the intended one -- 8BitDo
adapters, which do answer the first handshake, skip the baudrate command.
That regression was spotted in review by mcfbytes.

Verified on hardware: DE10-Nano (MiSTer FPGA, arm/socfpga) running
6.18.38, with a 1st-generation 8BitDo USB Wireless Adapter switched to
Switch mode (057e:2009) and an 8BitDo SN30 Pro+ connected to it.
Controlled A/B, identical hardware state in both arms (same adapter,
same adapter firmware, controller powered on and paired at enumeration
time, fresh replug before each arm); the kernel was the only variable:

  without the patch: 17 USB disconnects / 17 re-enumerations ~512 ms
                     apart, 13x "Failed to set baudrate (ret=-71)",
                     13x "Failed handshake; ret=-71", 18x "probe - fail",
                     HID device bound to no driver, no input device,
                     gamepad unusable
  with the patch:    0 disconnects / 1 enumeration, no failures,
                     "controller MAC = E4:17:D8:F9:77:78", HID device
                     bound to nintendo, Pro Controller and IMU input
                     devices created, battery reports "Discharging" read
                     over the air, gamepad works

The MAC read back is the adapter's own address, not the address of the
paired controller, which confirms the OUI prefix is a valid
discriminator on this hardware.

Note for anyone re-testing: an arm only exercises this patch if
joycon_read_info() succeeded -- check the log for a "controller MAC"
line. An arm that logs "Failed to get joycon info; ret=-110" never
reached the baudrate command and measured nothing, so the patched and
unpatched kernels look identical.

What makes read_info time out has not been isolated. It is not simply
the controller being powered off: across two reboots with the pad fully
powered down, read_info still answered within 2.5 s and the driver bound
normally. The MAC it returns is the adapter's own, so the adapter
answers the device-info subcommand out of its own firmware whether or
not the controller is awake.

